seg-31a173dfe40d = <b>A work directory.</b> The server reads and writes IQ files only inside the folder named by <c1/>. Every path argument is relative to it (an absolute path must resolve inside it); inputs are resolved through symlinks before the check, an output's folder must already exist inside it, and an existing output is replaced only with <c2/>. Unset, the IQ file tools are off and say how to enable them; every other tool is unaffected.

# text
# c1: KSHANA_MCP_IQ_MAX_SAMPLES
# c2: cf32_le
# c3: ci16_le
# c4: ci8
# c5: cf32_le
# c6: format
# c7: max_seconds
seg-4f734b84a91a = <b>A sample budget.</b> One call generates or processes at most <c1/> complex samples (default { "50 000 000" }). In bytes that is { "400" } MB as <c2/> ({ "8" } bytes a complex sample), { "200" } MB as <c3/> ({ "4" } bytes) and { "100" } MB as <c4/> ({ "2" } bytes); a scene is written as <c5/> unless <c6/> says otherwise. The check runs before any work, and the refusal names the duration or <c7/> that fits.

# text
# c1: json_out
# c2: csv_out
seg-178284ddfee0 = <b>Compact replies.</b> Each tool answers with one JSON summary: acquisition peaks, C/N0, lock state, and every file written with its path and byte count. Per-epoch tables go to the <c1/> / <c2/> files you name.

